BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze concord rules for indefinite quantifiers, fractions, percentages, and mass nouns in biochemical data reporting.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Proportional Concord Mechanics:) Tj
T*
(   With fractions, percentages, decimals, and indefinite quantifiers \(all, some, none, half, part,) Tj
T*
(   majority\), concord is determined not by the quantifier itself, but by the object of the) Tj
T*
(   preposition 'of' that follows:) Tj
T*
(   - Uncountable / Mass Noun Object -> Singular Verb:) Tj
T*
(   - "Fifty percent of the cell lysate was discarded.") Tj
T*
(   - "Two-thirds of the biomass has been consumed.") Tj
T*
(   - Countable Plural Noun Object -> Plural Verb:) Tj
T*
(   - "Fifty percent of the cultured colonies were resistant to ampicillin.") Tj
T*
(   - "Two-thirds of the mice have completed the trial.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. 'The Number of' vs. 'A Number of':) Tj
T*
(   - 'The number of': Emphasizes the specific mathematical count as a singular abstract figure ->) Tj
T*
(   Takes a singular verb:) Tj
T*
(   - "The number of false-positive assays was remarkably low.") Tj
T*
(   - 'A number of': Functions as an idiomatic plural quantifier meaning 'several' or 'many' ->) Tj
T*
(   Takes a plural verb:) Tj
T*
(   - "A number of false-positive assays were identified during validation.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 10:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Discuss inverted sentence structures \(negative inversion and locative inversion\) and their stylistic impact in technical discourse.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Negative Inversion \(Subject-Auxiliary Inversion\):) Tj
T*
(   When a negative or limiting adverbial expression begins a clause, the auxiliary verb is moved) Tj
T*
(   in front of the subject noun phrase \(identical to interrogative word order\):) Tj
T*
(   - Adverbial Triggers: rarely, seldom, hardly, scarcely, barely, neither, nor, not only, under) Tj
T*
(   no circumstances, at no time.) Tj
T*
(   - Example 1: "Seldom does a single point mutation confer complete multidrug resistance.") Tj
T*
(   - Example 2: "Not only did the enzyme cleave the synthetic substrate, but it also exhibited) Tj
T*
(   stereospecificity.") Tj
T*
(   - Rule: If no auxiliary verb exists in the affirmative statement, a 'do'-support auxiliary \(do,) Tj
T*
(   does, did\) must be supplied.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Locative Inversion \(Prepositional Inversion\):) Tj
T*
(   When a spatial prepositional phrase is placed sentence-initially for vivid thematic focus or) Tj
T*
(   given-new cohesion, the main verb moves before the subject:) Tj
T*
(   - Example: "Within the active catalytic site resides a conserved triad of catalytic residues.") Tj
T*
(   - Stylistic Value: Anchors physical laboratory or molecular spatial layout directly into reader) Tj
T*
(   cognition before revealing the acting element.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 11:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Examine the formation, polarity, and grammatical rules of question tags in professional communication and defense vivas.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Structural Anatomy of Question Tags:) Tj
T*
(   A question tag is a mini-question appended to the end of a declarative statement to seek) Tj
T*
(   confirmation, verify agreement, or maintain interactive academic dialogue.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

