(>> Solution to Question 22:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Discuss subject-verb concord in inverted locative and existential 'there' constructions in scientific descriptions.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Existential 'There' Constructions:) Tj
T*
(   In sentences beginning with the dummy pronoun 'there', 'there' is an expletive subject; the) Tj
T*
(   true syntactic subject follows the verb. The verb must agree in number with the post-verbal) Tj
T*
(   delayed subject:) Tj
T*
(   - Singular Delayed Subject:) Tj
T*
(   - "There was an unexpected drop in cell viability during the third passage.") Tj
T*
(   - Plural Delayed Subject:) Tj
T*
(   - "There were several anomalous bands visible on the agarose gel.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Compound Delayed Subjects:) Tj
T*
(   When the delayed subject is compound, formal syntax mandates plural agreement:) Tj
T*
(   - Formal: "There are a micropipette and a centrifuge tube on the clean bench.") Tj
T*
(   - Notional / Colloquial Proximity: Spoken English often uses "There is a micropipette and...",) Tj
T*
(   but this is considered ungrammatical in formal scientific manuscripts.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Locative Inversions:) Tj
T*
(   - "At the core of the ribosome lie two catalytic subunits." \(Subunits is plural -> lie\).) Tj
T*
(   - "Inside the mitochondrial matrix is located the citric acid cycle machinery." \(Machinery is) Tj
T*
(   uncountable singular -> is located\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 23:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Examine the syntax of elliptical clauses and the hazard of dangling elliptical clauses in technical methodology.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Nature of Elliptical Clauses:) Tj
T*
(   An elliptical clause is a dependent clause in which certain grammatical elements \(often the) Tj
T*
(   subject and a form of 'to be'\) are omitted because they are understood from context:) Tj
T*
(   - Full: "While the technician was preparing the master mix, she wore nitrile gloves.") Tj
T*
(   - Elliptical: "While preparing the master mix, she wore nitrile gloves.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. The Dangling Elliptical Clause:) Tj
T*
(   An elliptical clause dangles when its omitted subject is different from the grammatical subject) Tj
T*
(   of the adjacent independent clause:) Tj
T*
(   - Flawed: "While analyzing the microarray data, the computer crashed.") Tj
T*
(   - Analysis: The omitted subject is implied to be 'the computer' \("While the computer was) Tj
T*
(   analyzing..."\), which produces absurdity.) Tj
T*
(   - Flawed: "When diluted with water, the technician observed a color shift." \(The technician was) Tj
T*
(   not diluted with water\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Rectification:) Tj
T*
(   - Restore the explicit subject and verb in the dependent clause:) Tj
T*
(   - Correct: "While the bioinformatician was analyzing the microarray data, the computer) Tj
T*
(   crashed.") Tj
T*
(   - Correct: "When the reagent was diluted with water, a distinct color shift occurred.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET

endstream
endobj
