45 780 Td
(   nothing, hardly, scarcely, barely\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. The Double Negative Fallacy:) Tj
T*
(   Pairing two negative words within the same clause cancels the negation and creates an) Tj
T*
(   unintended affirmative, which is strictly unacceptable in formal scientific syntax:) Tj
T*
(   - Illogical: "The culture didn't show no signs of contamination.") Tj
T*
(   - Correct: "The culture showed no signs of contamination" OR "The culture did not show any) Tj
T*
(   signs of contamination.") Tj
T*
(   - Inadvertent Double Negatives with Semi-Negatives:) Tj
T*
(   - Illogical: "We couldn't hardly detect the fluorescent signal.") Tj
T*
(   - Correct: "We could hardly detect the fluorescent signal.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Litotes \(Deliberate Understatement in Academic Register\):) Tj
T*
(   Using a negative word paired with a negative prefix to express a qualified, cautious positive) Tj
T*
(   claim:) Tj
T*
(   - "The difference in binding affinity was not insignificant." \(Means the difference was) Tj
T*
(   meaningful, but stated with scholarly caution\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 27:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze the syntax of restrictive versus non-restrictive participial phrases in experimental results.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Participial Phrase Structural Functions:) Tj
T*
(   A participial phrase consists of a present or past participle plus any accompanying objects or) Tj
T*
(   modifiers, functioning adjectivally to modify a noun phrase.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Restrictive Participial Phrases:) Tj
T*
(   - Function: Identifies which specific entity is being discussed; essential to sentence meaning.) Tj
T*
(   - Punctuation: No commas.) Tj
T*
(   - Example: "Bacterial cells expressing green fluorescent protein fluoresced under ultraviolet) Tj
T*
(   illumination.") Tj
T*
(   - Analysis: Only those cells actively expressing GFP fluoresced. Removing the phrase alters the) Tj
T*
(   empirical claim.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Non-Restrictive Participial Phrases:) Tj
T*
(   - Function: Supplies descriptive background information about an entity already clearly) Tj
T*
(   identified.) Tj
T*
(   - Punctuation: Must be set off with commas.) Tj
T*
(   - Example: "Green fluorescent protein, originally isolated from Aequorea victoria, is a 27 kDa) Tj
T*
(   fluorophore.") Tj
T*
(   - Analysis: GFP is already uniquely defined; the phrase provides historical context.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 28:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Examine the rules governing verb concord with subjects modified by 'each', 'every', 'many a', and 'more than one'.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Distributive Subject Quantifiers:) Tj
T*
(   - Each and Every: Distributive adjectives that force a singular conceptual focus on individual) Tj
T*
(   members of a set -> Mandate singular verbs:) Tj
T*
(   - "Every pipette, tube, and flask was sterilized." \(Even with multiple compound nouns joined by) Tj
T*
(   'and', 'every' enforces a singular verb\).) Tj
T*
(   - "Each of the ninety-six wells was inoculated with 100 uL of culture.") Tj
T*
ET

endstream
